# Fixed defect 132's class, 2026-09-29, a mistake on a line joined to one
# that holds a token the lexer refused. Symptom: `x = 0X10 +` over
# `print(1) )` cost `base_prefix_case` and `continuation_outside_brackets`,
# and the `)` was told only once `check --apply` had written `0x10`: a
# certain fix wrote a program whose parse reported a code the first run had
# not; `€ +` over `print(2) )` hid its `)` the same way. Cause: the joined
# reading's recovery drops what follows the `error` token the lexer made of
# the refused token, and `parse/apart` weighed only the parser's own
# refusals, so it read the joined reading as having reached the `)` and
# said nothing. Now an `error` token counts as the refusal it is, in either
# reading, but a foreign word's the parser reads as its certain swap
# (`errors_in`), and the lines apart tell the `)`.

function a_malformed_literal_left_open()
    #~v base_prefix_case continuation_outside_brackets
    x = 0x10 +
    print(1) )  #~ expected_end_of_line
    print(x)

function a_refused_character_left_open()
    #~v unexpected_character continuation_outside_brackets
    € +
    print(2) )  #~ expected_end_of_line

function main()
    a_malformed_literal_left_open()
    a_refused_character_left_open()
